 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nToken response parsing no longer calculates expires_at from expires_in\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen parsing OAuth2 token responses that contain an `expires_in` field, the `expires_at` timestamp is no longer being calculated and added to the response parameters. This breaks token expiration handling for clients that rely on the computed `expires_at` value.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2.rfc6749.parameters import parse_token_response\nimport json\n\n# Token response with expires_in but no expires_at\ntoken_json = json.dumps({\n    \"access_token\": \"2YotnFZFEjr1zCsicMWpAA\",\n    \"token_type\": \"Bearer\", \n    \"expires_in\": 3600,\n    \"scope\": \"/profile\"\n})\n\nresponse = parse_token_response(token_json)\nprint(\"Response:\", response)\nprint(\"Has expires_at:\", 'expires_at' in response)\n```\n\nExpected: The response should contain an `expires_at` field calculated from the current time + `expires_in` seconds.\n\nActual: The response only contains the original `expires_in` field, with no `expires_at` calculated.\n\nThis affects all OAuth2 client types when parsing token responses that include expiration information.\n</issue>\n",
